From 42a624bf4e0e246e32d66481064c770e5acde0d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:35:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(movement): retain completed client prediction frames

Native 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 packets distinguish completed fluid motion
from air gravity. At eye Y 103.4815826 a native idle-water frame reports
-0.022315647 blocks per tick, while the previous air approximation reports
-0.0996117 for the matching translated position. Preserve a negotiated
client motion sample instead of reconstructing it from rounded positions.

Consume each sample once at CLIENT_TICK_END, verify that its feet position
matches the standard Java movement stream, and reject non-finite values,
unknown fields and incompatible protocol revisions. Server state and
vehicle prediction remain authoritative; ordinary Java clients retain
their existing approximation. Preserve the sampled floor motion: native
air-floor gameplay reports -0.0784, while water-floor motion is -0.005.

Track local swimming transitions from the same completed frame, including
ViaProxy clients. The client integration sends these frames after normal
Java movement and before the tick-end packet. Keep correction handling,
auth-input construction, frame validation and event state in core.

Carry completed horizontal and vertical collision axes with every prediction
frame, including stationary frames that Java omits from position updates.
Consume them only for a matching unmounted frame outside dimension changes.
Native 1.26.51.1 wall and open-water captures distinguish collision from
grounded state and clear horizontal collision on the release frame.

Apply corrected player velocity in all poses and retain zero-velocity resets.
Java relative velocity flags previously discarded this authority outside
gliding. Corrections without usable client history must still apply rather
than disappear due to a tick window check. This preserves authoritative
position and motion; input-history resimulation remains a separate gap.

Native 1.26.51.1 protocol 2193 pool-floor jumping holds Jumping, JumpDown
and WantUp without StartJumping. The old grounded-plus-held heuristic
sends a false StartJumping on the first water-rise frame even though
the full 29-frame position and motion sequence already matches native.

Accept the actual ground-jump impulse in completed prediction frames and
use it for StartJumping. Do not infer this event from motion thresholds,
held input or grounded state when the client supplies local physics.
Use a separately negotiated revision-five channel to prevent earlier
clients from sending an incompatible layout. Keep the standard Java
approximation for connections without a completed local frame.

Evaluate the native SneakTriggerIntent posture rules in core. The pinned
1.26.51.1 executable separates swimming, crawling and sneaking flags, probes
standing/crouching/crawling block space, and uses a 0.49999997F movement
threshold when crouching space is clear. Low ceilings switch swimming to
crawling outside body water and crawling back to swimming on water entry.
Keep these decisions independent from delayed server metadata.

Revision five transports all three completed posture flags. Emit swimming
and crawling edges once per connection, and distinguish effective sneaking
from physical SneakDown/WantDown input while preserving shield blocking.
The add-on supplies collision probes and local input; core owns the rule,
wire validation and Bedrock events. Metadata history rewind and input
resimulation remain incomplete.

Bypass posture input slowdown during flight, previous swimming, or water
contact for this frame. Matching 1.26.51.1 calculator 0x1404495e0 and its
adapter 0x14669d7d0 identify WasInWaterFlagComponent as the water bypass.
The movement request writer 0x148b58fe0 copies the processed coordinates.
Carry pre-input water contact in revision 5 so core packet construction
and client physics share the rule, including the crawl-to-water frame.
Reject incompatible revisions and exercise all 128 flag combinations.
